WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.967
Question 0004: three boxes with wrong labels,
a junior-level question.

2
00:00:04.967 --> 00:00:09.367
Two parts: how many draws are needed,
and how to write it in code.

3
00:00:10.167 --> 00:00:15.400
One box holds only red balls, another only black,
and the third holds both.

4
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:19.633
Each box has a label on it:
"Red", "Black", and "Red and Black".

5
00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:22.533
And the only rule: every label is wrong.

6
00:00:23.333 --> 00:00:28.533
Part A: what is the minimum number of draws
that guarantees we can fix all the labels?

7
00:00:29.233 --> 00:00:33.700
A draw: you point at a box,
and get one random ball from it.

8
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:38.833
There are six ways to assign the three kinds of content
to the three boxes.

9
00:00:38.833 --> 00:00:44.233
But every label is wrong, so we rule out
any arrangement where a box's content matches its label.

10
00:00:44.233 --> 00:00:48.467
Only two possible arrangements remain,
and we must decide between them.

11
00:00:48.467 --> 00:00:53.567
The "Red" box holds black or the mix.
A black ball fits both arrangements.

12
00:00:53.567 --> 00:00:58.233
The "Black" box holds the mix or red,
and a red ball fits both.

13
00:00:58.233 --> 00:01:02.967
Only in the "Red and Black" box
do the arrangements differ: red in one, black in the other.

14
00:01:02.967 --> 00:01:08.433
The idea: the "Red and Black" label is wrong,
so this box holds a single color only.

15
00:01:10.033 --> 00:01:15.433
Case one: the "Red and Black" box gives a red ball.
So it is the red box.

16
00:01:15.433 --> 00:01:20.933
The "Black" box is not black, since its label is wrong,
and not red either. So it holds the mix.

17
00:01:20.933 --> 00:01:24.433
And the "Red" box is left with black.

18
00:01:24.433 --> 00:01:28.933
Case two: a black ball comes out.
Then this is the black box.

19
00:01:28.933 --> 00:01:34.433
The "Red" box is neither red nor black,
so it holds the mix, and "Black" is left with red.

20
00:01:35.233 --> 00:01:40.733
The rule: color c came out? The box labeled c
holds the other color, and the third holds the mix.

21
00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:45.967
And why not zero? With no draw, two arrangements remain,
and there is no way to choose between them.

22
00:01:46.567 --> 00:01:50.867
So the answer: one draw,
and it must be from the "Red and Black" box.

23
00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:56.567
In the page's box you can try it:
click a box to draw a ball from it.

24
00:01:56.567 --> 00:02:00.367
We draw from the box "Red and Black",
and a red ball comes out.

25
00:02:00.367 --> 00:02:04.867
Revealing the contents shows exactly what we deduced:
all three labels, from one draw.

26
00:02:06.467 --> 00:02:12.433
The trap: drawing from the "Red" box.
A black ball from it leaves both arrangements open.

27
00:02:12.433 --> 00:02:17.400
The same goes for the "Black" box.
Only "Red and Black" is known in advance to hold one color.

28
00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:21.400
Part B: turning the solution into a function.

29
00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:27.600
The input: an array labels of three labels,
that is, RED, BLACK and MIXED, in some order.

30
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:32.800
The API: draw(i) takes a random ball from box i,
and returns RED or BLACK.

31
00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:38.300
The page's example uses arrangement 1 from part A,
so the ball from box 2 is red.

32
00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:44.100
The first solution translates part A almost word for word,
and builds a new array, fixed.

33
00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:50.600
The function takes the array of labels
and returns the correct labels.

34
00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:56.800
The array fixed will hold the corrected labels.
For now, three empty strings.

35
00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:05.300
First loop: find the box labelled MIXED
and keep its index in k.

36
00:03:06.633 --> 00:03:11.133
The only draw in the function, from box k.
That box holds one colour, so ball is its colour.

37
00:03:15.466 --> 00:03:19.966
The if sets the second colour, other:
BLACK if ball is RED, and RED otherwise.

38
00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:25.533
Box k gets the label ball,
the only colour inside it.

39
00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:34.700
Second loop: the box labelled ball
gets other, by the rule of Part A.

40
00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:43.900
Third loop: the box labelled other holds
neither other nor ball, so it is MIXED.

41
00:03:45.066 --> 00:03:48.966
And return fixed.
The array labels itself is unchanged.

42
00:03:49.666 --> 00:03:53.966
We run it on the page's example, beside the box,
with draw returning RED.

43
00:03:53.966 --> 00:03:58.966
The first pass looks for MIXED.
It is on box 2, so k = 2.

44
00:03:58.966 --> 00:04:03.100
One draw from box 2: it gives RED.

45
00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:08.100
The other color, other, is BLACK,
and box 2 gets RED.

46
00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:13.033
The second pass: the box labeled RED
gets other, that is, BLACK.

47
00:04:13.033 --> 00:04:17.966
The third pass: the box labeled BLACK
gets MIXED.

48
00:04:17.966 --> 00:04:21.566
The result: BLACK, MIXED, RED.
Exactly arrangement 1.

49
00:04:22.366 --> 00:04:28.966
And if the ball is black? other is RED,
and box 2 gets BLACK.

50
00:04:28.966 --> 00:04:34.866
Box 1 gets RED, and box 0 gets MIXED.
That is arrangement 2.

51
00:04:35.666 --> 00:04:40.900
Correct and readable, but it makes three passes
over the array and keeps an extra array.

52
00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:46.600
The second solution starts from an observation:
the fix is a rotation of the three labels.

53
00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:51.500
Call same the box labeled with the ball's color,
and other the third box.

54
00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:56.600
The ball's color moves to k, MIXED moves to other,
and the other color moves to same.

55
00:04:56.600 --> 00:05:00.333
And a rotation of three elements
is exactly two swaps.

56
00:05:01.133 --> 00:05:05.866
In the page's box, fix_labels runs step by step
on the page's example.

57
00:05:05.866 --> 00:05:11.000
The arrows over the boxes are the rotation
of the three labels; same and other are marked.

58
00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:16.133
At the end the box reveals the contents:
all three labels are right, after two swaps.

59
00:05:16.933 --> 00:05:21.333
The code: after the draw there is no second search,
and no new array.

60
00:05:22.833 --> 00:05:27.333
This time the function fixes labels in place
and returns the same array.

61
00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:35.100
The index method finds MIXED in one line,
and the only draw is again from box k.

62
00:05:37.533 --> 00:05:41.633
The neighbours of k on the circle, n1 and n2,
with no loop and no search.

63
00:05:46.666 --> 00:05:51.166
One check: the neighbour labelled with the ball's colour
is same, and the other one is other.

64
00:05:54.533 --> 00:05:59.033
The first swap, by multiple assignment, with no tmp:
box k gets the ball's colour.

65
00:06:03.166 --> 00:06:07.666
The second swap, between same and other.
Together the two swaps are the rotation.

66
00:06:08.933 --> 00:06:11.833
And return labels, fixed in place.

67
00:06:12.533 --> 00:06:16.566
And now the run beside the page's box,
again with a red ball.

68
00:06:16.566 --> 00:06:21.700
Again k = 2, and the ball that comes out is red.

69
00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:26.600
The neighbors of k: (k + 1) % 3, that is, 0,
and (k + 2) % 3, that is, 1.

70
00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:31.200
Box 0 says RED, like the ball,
so same = 0 and other = 1.

71
00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:36.866
First swap, k with same: box 2 is now correct,
and MIXED moves to 0 for the moment.

72
00:06:36.866 --> 00:06:42.000
Second swap, same with other:
the result is BLACK, MIXED, RED.

73
00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:46.033
The same result as the first solution,
with two swaps in place.

74
00:06:46.833 --> 00:06:52.333
And why (k + 2) % 3 and not (k − 1) % 3?
Both should give the previous neighbor on the circle.

75
00:06:52.333 --> 00:06:57.566
In Python, (0 − 1) % 3 is 2,
because there the result of % is never negative.

76
00:06:57.566 --> 00:07:03.066
In SystemVerilog and C, % keeps the sign of the dividend:
it gives −1, an invalid index.

77
00:07:03.066 --> 00:07:08.566
Adding 2 equals subtracting 1 modulo 3, and stays positive.
So the code is the same in both languages.

78
00:07:09.366 --> 00:07:13.100
The cost: both solutions are O(1),
with a single draw.

79
00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:17.400
The second makes one pass and two swaps in place,
with no extra array.

80
00:07:18.187 --> 00:07:23.220
To sum up: one draw from the "Red and Black" box
fixes all three labels.

81
00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:28.620
In code, the fix is a rotation: two swaps in place.
Try it in the box on the page of question 0004.
